# Local overrides for the Unisoc vendor_boot recovery environment.
#
# 1) SELinux: the stock policy is patched at build time so the init and
#    recovery domains are permissive. Writing enforce here only works because
#    of that patch - a plain `write` from a non-permissive init domain is
#    denied, which is why earlier attempts silently did nothing.
on early-init
    write /sys/fs/selinux/enforce 0

# 2) USB gadget ownership.
#
#    Two things compete for the gadget:
#      - TWRP's init.rc, which runs `mount functionfs adb /dev/usb-ffs/adb` in
#        its `on fs` block. That mount BLOCKS until the gadget is bound to a UDC.
#      - the stock Unisoc flow in init.recovery.common.rc, which configures and
#        binds the gadget only when all of these hold:
#             sys.usb.configfs=1
#             sys.usb.ffs.ready=1
#             sys.usb.config=adb,vser,gser
#
#    TWRP only ever sets `sys.usb.config=adb`, so with the vendor flow in
#    charge the gadget never binds, the functionfs mount never returns, init
#    stays blocked in `on fs`, adbd is never started and the panel never comes
#    up - black screen, no ADB, no reboot.
#
#    Publish the vendor's expected values early so the stock flow runs and the
#    mount can complete.
on init
    setprop sys.usb.configfs 1
    setprop sys.usb.config adb,vser,gser

#    Neither side starts adbd for the vendor string:
#      - TWRP only matches `on property:sys.usb.config=adb`
#      - init.recovery.common.rc binds the UDC but never mentions adbd
#    Start it explicitly so the daemon is running and simply waits for the
#    adb function to become enabled.
    start adbd
